---
import type { Locale } from '~/layers/types';
import { useTranslations, localePath } from '~/lib/i18n';

interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
}
const { locale } = Astro.props;
const t = useTranslations(locale);

const isIt = [
  {
    is: 'A map of public infrastructure, institutions, contracts and policy.',
    isNot: 'A map of people. No layer here names, describes, or tracks any individual.',
  },
  {
    is: 'A historical and periodic record of where things have been observed.',
    isNot: 'A live tracker. Nothing here updates in real time, and nothing shows where anyone is.',
  },
  {
    is: 'Sourced from public records, with every source cited and every gap stated.',
    isNot: 'Complete. Every layer is partial, dated, or approximate — each one says how.',
  },
  {
    is: 'Informational tools for making records requests and contacting officials.',
    isNot: 'Legal advice. If you need legal help, talk to a lawyer.',
  },
];
---

<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100 sm:text-3xl">{t('limitsTitle')}</h1>

  <p class="prose-civic mt-3">
    This project exists because the systems described here are public record and effectively
    invisible anyway — scattered across agency spreadsheets, permit filings, and university
    archives. Putting them on one map changes what a resident can see about their own street. That
    is worth doing carefully, so here is exactly what this is.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink-800">
    <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-ink-900 text-xs tracking-wider text-ink-400 uppercase">
        <tr>
          <th scope="col" class="w-1/2 px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold">What this is</th>
          <th scope="col" class="w-1/2 px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold">What it is not</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-ink-800">
        {
          isIt.map((row) => (
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="px-4 py-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-200">{row.is}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-300">{row.isNot}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-civic mt-10">
    <h2>Transparency for systems, privacy for people</h2>
    <p>
      The whole project turns on one line: we point at power and infrastructure, never at people.
      Cameras, agencies, facilities, contracts, buildings, and policy are fair subjects for public
      scrutiny — they are funded publicly, they exercise public authority, and their records are
      public. Individuals are not.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a hard product boundary, not a preference. There is no field in our data schema that
      could hold a personal name, a case number, or an identifier for a private individual, and
      there will not be one. Where an upstream dataset mixes individual records into systemic data,
      the ingest takes the systemic part and drops the rest. Detainee-level data is permanently out
      of scope — it will never enter this repository, this map, or any export from it.
    </p>

    <h2>We do not surveil our own users</h2>
    <p>
      There are no accounts, no trackers, no third-party analytics, no advertising technology, and
      no cookies on this site. There is no server-side application at all: the whole thing is
      static files. When you use the "near me" lookup, the place list is downloaded to your browser
      and the search runs there — nothing you type is sent anywhere, because there is nothing to
      send it to and nowhere to log it. If you use browser geolocation, the coordinates are read
      into memory and discarded.
    </p>
    <p>
      It would be incoherent to map surveillance infrastructure while quietly running surveillance
      infrastructure. So we don't.
    </p>

    <h2>About the camera layer specifically</h2>
    <p>
      The ALPR layer deserves its own warning. It is crowd-sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors,
      which means it is <strong>incomplete</strong>, <strong>historical</strong>, and
      <strong>probabilistic</strong>. A camera may have been removed, moved, or re-aimed since
      someone mapped it. A street with no dot may still be covered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read it as evidence that a surveillance network exists and roughly where it has been
      observed — never as a guide to where you are or are not being recorded.
    </p>

    <h2>Errors and takedowns</h2>
    <p>
      If a record here is wrong, we want to fix it. Every layer links to its upstream source, so
      corrections usually belong upstream — at OpenStreetMap for cameras, or with the publishing
      agency for the rest — and will flow here on the next refresh. For anything else, open an
      issue on the repository.
    </p>
    <p>
      Grassroots mapping of ALPR networks has drawn legal threats before; DeFlock received a
      cease-and-desist and refused it with EFF backing on First Amendment grounds. Our posture is
      to keep provenance clean, use only public data, publish the ingest code, and stay easy to
      mirror.
    </p>

    <h2>Licensing</h2>
    <p>
      The code is MIT. Data we compile ourselves is CC BY 4.0. Upstream layers keep their own
      terms, which are not ours to relax — the camera layer is ODbL, redlining is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0,
      and the federal records are public domain. The <a href={localePath(locale, '/data')}>open data
      page</a> lists which is which before you download anything.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
